WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:07.860
Hi.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.890
My name is Barret Schloerke,
and I'm a software engineer

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.290
on the Shiny Team at RStudio.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.680
Today, we're going to talk
about how plumber and future can

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.130
be combined to create
an asynchronous web API.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:24.570
So let's consider an example
where we have two R functions.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.330
Each function takes an
ID, but the first one

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.310
would do a fast calculation.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.159
We can think it'll
take roughly 0 seconds.

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.770
And the second one will
do a slow calculation.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.040
This one will take
about 10 seconds.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.500
Plumber can take
these two functions

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.640
and decorate them using
roxygen2 like comments.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.250
In this case, both
of these functions

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.680
can be accessed
using a get route,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.490
but the first one will
be accessed under fast ID

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:57.030
and the second one will
be accessed using slow ID.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.730
Any questions?

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.330
The ID will represent the order
in which the request was sent.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:09.690
So if ID 1 will be
sent before ID 2,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.870
let's see how plumber will
handle these requests.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.300
In the receive
stage, the request

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.840
will be unpacked and be
prepped for processing.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.300
In the processing stage, we
will execute the user function,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.410
your function such as
the fast calculation

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.540
or the slow calculation.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.970
And then finally,
once they are done,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.170
we will bundle them up
in the response stage.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.120
The receive in
response stage must

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.390
be handled by plumber because
that is very specific plumber

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.010
logic.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.860
So let's look at
an example where

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.600
we have four
requests that arrive

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.640
at roughly the same time.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.230
In order, it will go a fast,
then a slow, then a slow,

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.860
and then a fast.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.850
Let's see how these execute.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.330
Fast comes in, is
processed really quickly,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.010
responded, and then done.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:06.630
Slow comes in, is
received, processed,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.070
but processing takes 10 seconds.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.370
It is a long time.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.810
A very long time.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:13.950
But that's OK.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.800
That's how long that
route takes, and then

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.870
will finally respond.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.600
The second slow route has
been waiting patiently,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.310
but will also take another
10 seconds to calculate.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.410
Little long, but it's OK.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:32.820
That's how long it takes, and
then finally it will respond.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.670
And the fast route has
been waiting patiently,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.310
processes really quickly,
and then responds

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.260
its original request.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:48.000
So in total, this will
take about 20 seconds.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.040
The first fast route will be
processed roughly instantly.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.160
The second request,
which is a slow route

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.360
will take 10 seconds to execute,
and then it will respond.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.720
But since R cannot process
multiple things at once,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.290
slow 3 will have to wait
10 seconds before it can be

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.850
processed.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.870
And finally, fast 4 had to
wait a total of 20 seconds

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.520
before it could
even be ingested.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.460
In our current situation,
all four requests

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.740
completed in 20 seconds.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.490
However, we accumulated
30 seconds of wait time.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.910
Ideally, we would
like this to be zero.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.490
Let's explore how future
can solve this for us.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.890
So here we have our
existing plumber API code.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.260
This is what it would look
like if we incorporated it

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.000
with future.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.540
Future is an R package
made by Henrik,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.000
which allows for parallel
and distributed computing

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.880
in R. Let's talk about
the three lines of code

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.640
that have been added
to our plumber API.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.060
The first line of
code tells Future

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.680
what plans should be used.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.605
In this case, we're going
to use a multi-session plan.

00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:07.480
I'll leave it to you to
figure out which plan

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.150
is best for your situation.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.760
The remaining code is
used to tell plumber

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.580
that, hey, this expression
needs to be calculated

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.279
using the future R package.

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:22.800
This allows for the main R
session to not be blocked.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.330
A point of note, must return
the results of your future call

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.670
in your plumber router
for it to take effect.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.180
Otherwise plumber will
not know what's happening.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.030
Let's take a look at
the execution time

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.550
with plumber and future.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.790
With just plumber alone,
it took 20 seconds total,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.150
and we had 30 seconds
of accumulated time.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:50.040
However, when we add in future,
everything is run immediately.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.080
This allows for the
total execution time

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.040
to only take 10 seconds, and
we have zero accumulated wait

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.000
time.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.180
This is the ideal
situation, and everything is

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.705
responding as fast as possible.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.840
Fast 4 responded
20 seconds earlier,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.460
and slow 3 also responded
10 seconds earlier.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.410
This is very good deal.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.820
Let's dive deep to see how
this execution is done.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.670
Plumber has the three steps
that we talked about earlier--

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.460
receive, process, and respond.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.160
This is all done on
the main R session.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.270
Only one route can
be there at a time.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.240
Future, however, can
launch multiple R sessions,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.390
and we'll call them
worker 1 and worker 2.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.130
In our use case,
we're only going

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.400
to be using them in
the processing step.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.420
So we must receive and respond
from the main plumber R

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.540
session, but we can
offload the processing

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.780
to the future workers.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.780
Let's watch to
see how this goes.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.830
Fast comes in, is
processed, and we respond.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:00.640
Nothing new.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:01.690
No surprise.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:05.230
Slow 2 is offloaded
immediately to worker 1.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.930
Since the main session
is free, then slow 3

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:09.850
can be offloaded to worker 2.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.030
This allows fast 4 to
be processed and respond

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:14.530
immediately.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.370
Once slow 2 is done, it can
respond same with slow 3.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.660
That's a lot faster and
a lot more going on.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:26.980
But in the end, we
have no wait time.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.630
Let's look at that again.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:33.890
This allows fast 4, since it's
not being blocked by slow 2

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:37.520
or slow 3, fast 4 can
respond immediately

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.866
at the zero second mark.

00:06:39.866 --> 00:06:43.250
And since we can do
things in parallel, slow 3

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:47.150
is also being processed at
the same time as slow 2.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.970
Both of these do not
block the main R session.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:50.720
This is ideal.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.350
However, there are
some limitations

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.750
what we can do with
plumber and future.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.780
One, you have to manually
add your future call

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.030
where we deem appropriate.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.160
This is because
adding the future call

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.000
adds a little bit of
execution time to each route.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.080
Now, if your execution time
is normally in the seconds,

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.480
and adding a quarter
of a second does not

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.190
mean much to you, then great.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.990
I think future is a perfect
candidate for that route.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.500
However, if adding a quarter
of a second to your execution

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.380
time dramatically
increases it, maybe

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.700
adding future maybe
not the best idea.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.815
And instead, will just keep
it in the main R session.

00:07:30.815 --> 00:07:32.190
The other one we
have to remember

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:35.880
is that processing power is
finite and memory is finite.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.350
We cannot necessarily launch
100,000 workers on one laptop.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.480
That would be a
little bit of overload

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.890
either with processing
power or the memory.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:49.320
One of the two will limit what
you can do on your machine.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.180
I'd like to thank
you for listening in

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.540
on how plumber and future Can
be integrated to help you create

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.550
an asynchronous web API.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.710
Remember, offload
your slow routes

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.050
using plumber
expressions, and this

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.800
will help keep your main plumber
worker available to process

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.720
incoming requests.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.630
Thank you.

